1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:04,720
From September 2024 to February 2026,

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:10,240
SARS approved about R79 billion in
withdrawals from South Africans' retirement

3
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:15,200
savings. And the tax on that
money came to about R21 billion.

4
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,080
That's the two-pot retirement system.

5
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:22,240
It lets you take some of your retirement
money early, without resigning.

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,320
But it's easy to get wrong,

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,160
and most people don't know what
they'll really get in their hand.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,360
In this video, you'll learn
how the three pots work,

9
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:35,040
what a withdrawal really
costs you, the traps to avoid,

10
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:38,560
and how to withdraw the
right way, if you need to.

11
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,240
Let's start from zero.

12
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,880
First, why did the rules change?

13
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:45,080
Before September 2024,

14
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:48,933
most people had only one way to
get their retirement money early.

15
00:00:48,987 --> 00:00:53,840
They had to resign. Leave your job,
and you could cash out your whole fund.

16
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,840
Many people did exactly that.

17
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:01,360
They changed jobs, took the cash, and
reached retirement with almost nothing saved.

18
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,813
So the government changed the rules.

19
00:01:03,867 --> 00:01:08,027
The two-pot system started
on 1 September 2024.

20
00:01:08,053 --> 00:01:12,000
The idea is simple: give you a
little access for emergencies,

21
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:15,720
and lock the rest away, so it's
still there when you retire.

22
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:20,853
Now, the pots. It's called two-pot…
but there are actually three.

23
00:01:20,907 --> 00:01:26,440
The vested pot. This is everything
you saved up to 31 August 2024.

24
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:29,547
It doesn't get new contributions,
but it keeps growing,

25
00:01:29,573 --> 00:01:31,973
and the old rules still apply to it.

26
00:01:32,027 --> 00:01:34,400
The savings pot. From 1 September 2024,

27
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,360
one third of your contributions go here.

28
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:41,947
This is the pot you can take
money from before you retire.

29
00:01:41,973 --> 00:01:45,400
The retirement pot.
The other two thirds go here.

30
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,200
This money is locked until you retire.

31
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:49,480
Think of it like this.

32
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,520
The savings pot is a small emergency door.

33
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,387
The retirement pot is a locked safe.

34
00:01:55,413 --> 00:02:00,280
To get things started, funds moved some
seed money into everyone's savings pot.

35
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:05,813
It was a once-off 10% of your vested
pot, up to a maximum of R30,000.

36
00:02:05,867 --> 00:02:07,720
It doesn't happen every year.

37
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:10,427
So, what are the rules
for the savings pot?

38
00:02:10,453 --> 00:02:13,093
You can only withdraw once per tax year.

39
00:02:13,147 --> 00:02:16,587
The tax year runs from 1
March to the end of February.

40
00:02:16,613 --> 00:02:19,973
And the smallest amount
you can take is R2,000.

41
00:02:20,027 --> 00:02:21,960
But here's what many people miss.

42
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,347
A withdrawal is not tax-free.

43
00:02:24,373 --> 00:02:28,600
It's added to your income for the year,
and taxed at your normal tax rate.

44
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:33,093
Your fund also charges an
admin fee, often around R300.

45
00:02:33,147 --> 00:02:38,080
And if you owe SARS money, SARS takes
that first, before you see a cent.

46
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:41,680
And remember, you don't
have to withdraw at all.

47
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:43,840
If you leave your savings pot alone,

48
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:46,800
it simply keeps growing
with the rest of your fund.

49
00:02:47,669 --> 00:02:48,869
Let's make that real.

50
00:02:49,429 --> 00:02:54,309
Say you earn R25,000 a month,
so R300,000 a year.

51
00:02:54,949 --> 00:02:59,589
You're under 65, and you withdraw
R20,000 from your savings pot.

52
00:03:00,229 --> 00:03:04,069
At that income, your tax rate is 26%.

53
00:03:04,189 --> 00:03:08,949
So SARS takes about
R5,200 of your R20,000.

54
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:12,789
Take off a fee of about R345,

55
00:03:12,829 --> 00:03:17,269
and you're left with around
R14,450 in your hand.

56
00:03:17,909 --> 00:03:20,709
So you didn't really get R20,000.

57
00:03:21,029 --> 00:03:24,629
You got about R14,450.

58
00:03:24,709 --> 00:03:26,389
And there's a hidden cost.

59
00:03:27,029 --> 00:03:29,909
If that R20,000 had stayed invested,

60
00:03:29,949 --> 00:03:33,109
growing at 5% a year above inflation,

61
00:03:33,229 --> 00:03:37,829
in 30 years it could be worth
about R86,000 in today's money.

62
00:03:38,429 --> 00:03:41,669
That's what the withdrawal
really costs your future self.

63
00:03:41,909 --> 00:03:44,309
It's only an illustration, not a promise.

64
00:03:44,869 --> 00:03:46,549
Now, the retirement pot.

65
00:03:47,029 --> 00:03:51,109
This one is strict. You can't
take it out before you retire.

66
00:03:51,269 --> 00:03:53,749
Not if you resign.
Not if you're retrenched.

67
00:03:54,069 --> 00:03:57,109
It stays in the fund,
or it moves to another fund.

68
00:03:57,669 --> 00:04:02,389
When you retire, it has to buy you
a monthly income, called an annuity.

69
00:04:03,109 --> 00:04:04,549
There are a few exceptions.

70
00:04:04,869 --> 00:04:07,669
If you pass away, it goes
to your beneficiaries.

71
00:04:08,149 --> 00:04:09,269
If you emigrate,

72
00:04:09,309 --> 00:04:13,429
you can get it after three years
in a row as a non-resident for tax.

73
00:04:13,909 --> 00:04:16,629
And if your savings are
small when you retire,

74
00:04:16,709 --> 00:04:18,869
you may be able to take it all in cash.

75
00:04:19,349 --> 00:04:21,189
Your fund can tell you if you qualify.

76
00:04:21,749 --> 00:04:26,629
What about the vested pot,
the money from before September 2024?

77
00:04:26,709 --> 00:04:28,789
The old rules still apply.

78
00:04:29,109 --> 00:04:32,309
If you're in a pension or
provident fund and you resign,

79
00:04:32,389 --> 00:04:35,589
you can usually still
cash out your vested pot.

80
00:04:35,669 --> 00:04:41,269
It's taxed, but the first R27,500
you withdraw in your lifetime is

81
00:04:41,349 --> 00:04:43,989
tax-free. And when you retire,

82
00:04:44,069 --> 00:04:48,949
up to R550,000 of your cash
lump sum can be tax-free.

83
00:04:49,349 --> 00:04:52,069
If you have a retirement
annuity, it's stricter.

84
00:04:52,549 --> 00:04:56,309
In most cases, you can't
touch it before age 55.

85
00:04:56,805 --> 00:05:01,269
Now, the four traps.
Trap one: the tax surprise.

86
00:05:01,589 --> 00:05:04,309
Your fund takes tax off when it pays you.

87
00:05:04,389 --> 00:05:08,309
But your final tax is worked out
when you file your tax return.

88
00:05:08,389 --> 00:05:12,629
If you have other income,
you could still owe SARS more later.

89
00:05:12,749 --> 00:05:15,109
Trap two: the flat fee.

90
00:05:15,189 --> 00:05:19,429
A fee of R320 is small on R20,000.

91
00:05:19,469 --> 00:05:25,109
But on a R2,000 withdrawal, that
same fee eats 16% of your money.

92
00:05:25,269 --> 00:05:27,269
Trap three: the habit.

93
00:05:27,389 --> 00:05:29,829
Once it's easy, people keep coming back.

94
00:05:29,909 --> 00:05:35,669
At Momentum, 62% of the
claims since 1 March 2026 were

95
00:05:35,829 --> 00:05:37,349
people's third withdrawal.

96
00:05:37,909 --> 00:05:40,949
Every time, your retirement gets smaller.

97
00:05:41,269 --> 00:05:47,109
Trap four: scams. Only apply through
your fund's official website or app,

98
00:05:47,189 --> 00:05:48,949
or through HR at work.

99
00:05:49,589 --> 00:05:54,869
Never share your OTP or your password
with anyone who offers to help you claim.

100
00:05:55,758 --> 00:05:57,518
So, should you withdraw?

101
00:05:57,998 --> 00:06:01,158
The savings pot is meant
for real emergencies.

102
00:06:01,358 --> 00:06:03,278
Not holidays. Not shopping.

103
00:06:03,598 --> 00:06:04,958
Not things that can wait.

104
00:06:05,438 --> 00:06:10,798
But life happens. If the choice is between
a withdrawal and a loan shark charging

105
00:06:10,958 --> 00:06:14,638
huge interest, a withdrawal
can be the lesser evil.

106
00:06:15,198 --> 00:06:17,598
Just know the real cost before you do it.

107
00:06:18,078 --> 00:06:21,198
Before you withdraw,
ask yourself three questions.

108
00:06:21,678 --> 00:06:23,598
Is this a real emergency?

109
00:06:24,078 --> 00:06:27,038
Is there a cheaper option,
like a payment plan?

110
00:06:27,118 --> 00:06:31,038
And do I know exactly what
I'll get after tax and fees?

111
00:06:31,598 --> 00:06:36,318
The best protection is your own emergency
fund, in a normal savings account.

112
00:06:36,398 --> 00:06:41,118
Even a small one. That way, your
retirement money can keep growing.

113
00:06:41,198 --> 00:06:43,598
If you do need to withdraw, here's how.

114
00:06:44,158 --> 00:06:48,718
Step one. Check that you have at
least R2,000 in your savings pot,

115
00:06:48,758 --> 00:06:51,358
and that you haven't
withdrawn yet this tax year.

116
00:06:51,678 --> 00:06:56,878
Step two. Make sure you're registered
for tax, your tax returns are all filed,

117
00:06:56,918 --> 00:07:00,878
and your name, ID number and
bank details match everywhere.

118
00:07:01,278 --> 00:07:03,438
If they don't, SARS can reject it.

119
00:07:03,998 --> 00:07:09,598
Step three. Apply through your fund's
website or app, or through HR at work.

120
00:07:09,918 --> 00:07:13,758
Step four. Your fund asks
SARS for a tax directive.

121
00:07:14,078 --> 00:07:18,558
That tells the fund how much tax to
take off, and any money you owe SARS.

122
00:07:19,118 --> 00:07:22,798
Step five. The fund pays the
rest into your bank account.

123
00:07:23,198 --> 00:07:26,478
Many funds pay within about two
weeks, but some take longer.

124
00:07:27,038 --> 00:07:30,638
And one tip: don't apply at
the very end of February.

125
00:07:30,958 --> 00:07:33,998
A late claim might count
against the next tax year.

126
00:07:34,558 --> 00:07:38,878
What's changing? Government is talking
about letting retrenched workers get some

127
00:07:38,958 --> 00:07:42,318
money from the retirement pot,
if they're in serious hardship.

128
00:07:42,718 --> 00:07:44,998
But right now, it's only a proposal.

129
00:07:45,118 --> 00:07:49,198
It is not the law. So be careful
of anyone who tells you otherwise.

130
00:07:49,758 --> 00:07:54,718
Let's recap. There are three pots:
vested, savings and retirement.

131
00:07:55,198 --> 00:07:59,998
You can withdraw from the savings
pot once a tax year, from R2,000.

132
00:08:00,078 --> 00:08:04,678
Every withdrawal is taxed, has a
fee, and costs you future growth.

133
00:08:04,958 --> 00:08:08,078
The retirement pot is
locked until you retire.

134
00:08:08,238 --> 00:08:11,198
And always claim through
official channels.

135
00:08:11,278 --> 00:08:14,158
If this helped,
subscribe to Money From Zero.

136
00:08:14,238 --> 00:08:18,238
New Shorts every day, and a
full guide like this every week.

137
00:08:18,398 --> 00:08:19,678
See you in the next one.

